{
  "title": "ElevenLabs audio tags for narration (Eleven v4 / Eleven v4 Turbo)",
  "verified": "2026-11-19",
  "sources": {
    "v4_docs": "https://elevenlabs.io/docs/best-practices/prompting/eleven-v4",
    "tags_list": "https://elevenlabs.io/blog/elevenlabs-audio-tags-list",
    "v3_tags": "https://elevenlabs.io/blog/v3-audiotags"
  },
  "refresh": "Re-read the three sources (WebFetch) when this file is more than ~60 days old, when a tag behaves unexpectedly, or when the user changes model. Update entries and 'verified'; keep 'measured_on_this_setup' as measured.",
  "models": ["eleven_v4", "eleven_v4_turbo"],
  "how_agents_use_this": "This file is information, not a template. 05-voice-and-edit.md -> 'Inline audio tags' is how a video's agents decide which tags, if any, fit THIS story's creative strategy (02), its pause intent (03) and the references' measured delivery (01, narration-measure). plan-check refuses only tags whose pipeline_status is not_for_narration and broken brackets, and adds each tag's time to the forecast; everything else is the agents' call. A tag not listed here is allowed ('custom') and judged by ear on the calibration chunk.",
  "how_tags_behave": [
    "A tag is written in square brackets inline in the sent text, before the words it should colour (or right after a phrase it closes). It is sent exactly as written; never parentheses or curly braces.",
    "A tag's emotion carries through the rest of its line; add a new tag only where the delivery should change (tags_list).",
    "One tag per clause: contrasting tags on the same words blur the performance. Several qualities go inside one bracket, comma-separated: [tense, cautious] (tags_list).",
    "Open vocabulary: any clear natural-language instruction can be a tag ([hushed and reverent, like a nature documentary narrator]). Describe the voice quality, not something that sounds like a sound cue, or it may be rendered as a sound effect (v4_docs).",
    "Voice-dependent: tags land readily when the delivery is in the voice's training data; outside it they are 'less reliable, and the result may not be optimal' (v4_docs). A serious voice may not respond to playful tags; a voice that conflicts with a tag can read it ALOUD as text (v3_tags). A malformed or unsupported tag is read aloud (tags_list).",
    "No SSML <break> tags on v4. Pauses come from pause tags, punctuation and text structure (v4_docs, v3_tags).",
    "Punctuation shapes pacing without any tag: comma = short pause; ellipsis = trailing pause with weight; dash = a beat; full stop = sentence rhythm; CAPITALS = emphasis; a line break = a longer beat (v4_docs, v3_tags).",
    "Eleven v4 performs better with more context; up to 10,000 characters per request (tags_list).",
    "Iterate: if a delivery misses, swap a neighbouring emotion and regenerate; results vary, test the specific tags and phrasing (v4_docs, tags_list)."
  ],
  "pipeline_rules": [
    "not_for_narration (refused by plan-check): distress tags (02: extreme distress is never shown or told), crude/novelty tags (family audience), sound-effect tags (sound design lives on the edit's own sound layer where it can be levelled, moved or removed; baked into the voice track it can only be fixed by re-recording, and the docs say tags should describe the voice), accents and character voices (one consistent narrator persona; caricature risk), dialogue tags (single narrator).",
    "A tag never forces overall pace: measured here, pace tags did not change the sustained rate over a real chunk (see measured_on_this_setup).",
    "A tag never replaces writing: a line that needs a tag to land usually needs rewriting first.",
    "Every tag the agents keep has a one-line story reason in editorial-review.md, and its real time cost is counted: approx_cost_s here until narration-measure has measured this voice (voice-lock.json -> measured.tag_costs_s)."
  ],
  "measured_on_this_setup": [
    "2026-11-19, eleven_v4: a pace tag prepended to every ~8,000-character chunk ([quick, measured pace]; [quickly, steady narration]) made no measurable difference to the real rate (185-187 WPM both times) although each measured 199-203 WPM on a short probe.",
    "2026-11-19, eleven_v4, voice UNbOi86R1a0f3ypyTCp4: [long pause] + [slightly pause] in one 56-line chunk added 12.3 s against the untagged take; five tags (2 [long pause], 2 [pause], 1 [slightly pause]) added 19.7 s to the full narration. Separate generations, so per-tag values are approximate: [long pause] roughly 6-8 s, [pause] ~2 s, [slightly pause] ~1-2 s. narration-measure measures the real gap at every tag of a real take; prefer those numbers.",
    "2026-11-19: the pov-military-narrative references (V017, V018) pause little: average gap between spoken segments 0.23-0.40 s, 90th percentile 0.40-0.70 s, longest 1.3-2.0 s, at most 1.2 gaps over 0.8 s per minute. A [long pause] is three to six times longer than anything those narrators do.",
    "2026-11-19: script lines joined with newlines vs spaces gave overall 167.3 vs 168.9 WPM on the same voice (different passages): about 1%, not a meaningful pace lever.",
    "2026-11-19: ElevenLabs' v4 prompting page lists a speed setting (0.7-1.2, default 1.0) separate from tags. Not tested here: the user's standing decision is v4/v4 Turbo with no speed setting; the tool accepts only stability and similarity_boost for these models."
  ],
  "statuses": {
    "usable": "Fits single-narrator documentary narration when the story's own moment calls for it.",
    "use_with_care": "Register- and voice-dependent: listen to the calibration chunk; one-off moments; never at a moment of suffering; never mocking a real person or group.",
    "not_for_narration": "Excluded by a pipeline rule (see pipeline_rules); plan-check refuses it."
  },
  "tags": [
    {"tag": "[pause]", "approx_cost_s": 2.0, "category": "pause", "effect": "Inserts a pause.", "example": "[pause]Then the phone rang.", "source": "tags_list, v3_tags", "pipeline_status": "usable", "notes": "Measured ~2 s here (approximate). About the length of the niche references' longest pauses."},
    {"tag": "[pauses]", "approx_cost_s": 2.0, "category": "pause", "effect": "A pause, as the docs' example spelling.", "source": "v4_docs", "pipeline_status": "usable", "notes": "Variant spelling; exact spelling may matter."},
    {"tag": "[short pause]", "approx_cost_s": 1.5, "category": "pause", "effect": "A short pause.", "source": "v4_docs (enhancement list)", "pipeline_status": "usable", "notes": "Grammatical alternative to [slightly pause]."},
    {"tag": "[brief pause]", "approx_cost_s": 1.5, "category": "pause", "effect": "A brief pause.", "source": "v4_docs", "pipeline_status": "usable", "notes": "Docs capitalise it [Brief pause]; matching here is case-insensitive."},
    {"tag": "[slightly pause]", "approx_cost_s": 1.5, "category": "pause", "effect": "A small hitch.", "source": "tags_list (spelled this way)", "pipeline_status": "usable", "notes": "Measured ~1-2 s here (approximate)."},
    {"tag": "[long pause]", "approx_cost_s": 7.0, "category": "pause", "effect": "A long pause.", "source": "tags_list, v4_docs (enhancement list)", "pipeline_status": "use_with_care", "notes": "Measured roughly 6-8 s here: longer than any silence in the niche references. A closing hold after the last line belongs to the edit (the last still held), not to this tag."},
    {"tag": "[pause, then normally]", "approx_cost_s": 2.0, "category": "pause", "effect": "A pause, then the normal delivery resumes.", "source": "v4_docs", "pipeline_status": "usable"},
    {"tag": "[gentle pause, then quiet realization]", "approx_cost_s": 2.0, "category": "pause", "effect": "A soft pause into a quiet, realizing delivery.", "source": "v4_docs", "pipeline_status": "usable"},

